  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n- Fall Storm System to bring Wind, Rain and Lightning to the area\n\nLow pressure is centered just east of Des Moines Iowa at 18Z with\nwidespread rain located to the north of the low over Minnesota and\nWisconsin. Two upper shortwaves from both the northern stream and\nsouthern stream are phasing off to the west over the upper midwest\nat this time. As such, the low will deepen as it moves east\nthrough the Lower Peninsula. Widespread/deep lift will sweep\nthrough Southwest Lower Michigan between roughly 400pm this\nafternoon and 400am tonight. The bulk of the rain will fall during\nthis period. The lift is driven by the right entrance region of an\nupper jet that will be located overhead which will drive a low\nlevel response of strong isentropic lift and significant moisture\ntransport. \n\nSounding climatology for DTX shows the max PWATs for today being \n1.55 inches. We will be north of 1.75 inches this evening. Given \nabnormal moisture in the depth of the column tonight we expect \nefficient rainfall production. Most areas will see 0.50 to 1.00 \namounts. Amounts may exceed an inch up towards Ludington. Given\nlow river levels and amounts that are not overly concerning not\nexpecting flooding issues. \n\nThunderstorms are a possibility this evening, especially along\nI-94 between 800pm and 200am. The instability is not strong,\nseveral hundred j/kg, but given the dynamics, thunder is a\ndistinct possibility. \n\nWind is the next concern and we have Gale Warnings out on the big\nlake. As for the land, we feel we will stay just below wind\nadvisory criteria with most gusts in the 30-40 mph range. It will\nvery much feel like fall with a wet, windy and rainy system\nworking through the state. \n\nThe second portion of the event will come on Friday as a strong\ncold front sweeps south around daybreak. Showers will accompany\nthe front and usher in cold enough air for lake effect rain.\nMidday into the afternoon CAPE over the lake is maximized, pushing\ntowards 1,000 j/kg. We are likely looking at thunderstorms off the\nlake given the significant moisture boost this time of the year \nvia the warmer water. Some diurnally driven showers and storms\nwill be possible inland as well given the cold air aloft. The most\nconcentrated rain will be working down the length of the lake\nthough in northerly flow. \n\n- Rather benign weather expected this weekend into early next week\n\nQuiet weather conditions will persist over the weekend into early\nnext week as high pressure dominates the scene both at the surface\nand aloft. \n\nThe main item of note is that frost and freezing temperatures are \npossible both Friday night and Saturday night.",
